Third-party framework citations

This skill references and credits third-party facilitation frameworks
and methods. Section A lists those that are named and linked only,
with none of their structure, principle wording, worksheets or
facilitation sequences restated. Section B lists established formats
in general facilitation practice that this skill describes in its own
words, with the originator and the write-up we consulted both
credited.

SECTION A. Named and linked only, not reproduced:

- Community Canvas (community-canvas.org, Fabian Pfortmüller, Nico
  Luchsinger, and Sascha Mombartz, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)
- Community Weaving (community-weaving.org, Fabian Pfortmüller, Michel
  Bachmann, Erin Dixon, and Sita Magnuson, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)
- Wenger's Communities of Practice (Etienne Wenger, "Communities of
  Practice: Learning, Meaning, and Identity," Cambridge University
  Press, 1998; Etienne Wenger, Richard McDermott, and William M.
  Snyder, "Cultivating Communities of Practice: A Guide to Managing
  Knowledge," Harvard Business School Press, 2002; copyrighted book
  content, not Creative Commons licensed)
- Liberating Structures (liberatingstructures.com, Henri Lipmanowicz
  and Keith McCandless, CC BY-NC 4.0, non-commercial use only)
- Open Space Technology (openspaceworld.org, Harrison Owen). The
  format is described and its purpose explained; its named principles
  and its guiding rule on participant movement are not restated, and
  readers are directed to Owen's own materials.
- Hyper Island Toolbox (toolbox.hyperisland.com), including the
  IDOARRT meeting-design worksheet, named as further reading only.
  Their check-in material is credited under Section B.
- SessionLab (sessionlab.com), named as a method library. Several of
  their write-ups are credited as consulted sources under Section B.
- The Marshmallow Challenge (Tom Wujec,
  tomwujec.com/marshmallowchallenge), named as further reading only.

This skill points users to those official sources for the full
material and carries its own original tools alongside them. For
community work: `assets/community-quick-read.md` and
`assets/community-audit-workshop.md`. For rapid participation: six
of the eight methods under "Rapid Participation Methods" in
`references/facilitation-methods.md`. Think, Pair, Room and The
Headline and the Cause are adaptations of established formats, both
credited under Section B below. For meetings and teams: three of the
five methods under "Meeting & Team Methods" in the same file, plus
`assets/opening-contract-template.md`. One Moment Each and The
Middle First are likewise adaptations, credited under Section B.
None of the originals require the external sources to be useful, and
none of them are derived from those sources.

SECTION B. Established formats described in our own words, with
origin and consulted write-up credited. These are long-standing
formats in general facilitation practice rather than proprietary
frameworks, and `references/facilitation-methods.md` carries this
skill's own description and step sequence for each. Where a published
write-up informed ours, it is cited in the method's Source line:

- World Café (originated by Juanita Brown and David Isaacs,
  theworldcafe.com; write-up consulted: sessionlab.com)
- Fishbowl discussion (general facilitation practice; write-up
  consulted: sessionlab.com)
- Mastermind groups (the concept originates with Napoleon Hill,
  "Think and Grow Rich," 1937; write-up consulted: voltagecontrol.com)
- Show and Tell / demo sessions (general practice; write-up
  consulted: sessionlab.com)
- Check-ins and check-outs (general practice; write-up consulted:
  toolbox.hyperisland.com)
- The Kaospilot Learning Arch, SET / HOLD / LAND (developed by the
  Kaospilot school, kaospilot.dk), used as the sequencing spine in
  `SKILL.md` and `assets/run-of-show-template.md`
- Think-Pair-Share (developed by Frank Lyman, 1981; write-up
  consulted: kent.edu/ctl/think-pair-share), adapted in this skill as
  Think, Pair, Room
- The human spectrogram (general facilitation practice; write-up
  consulted: sessionlab.com), adapted in this skill as The Middle
  First
- The premortem technique (developed by Gary Klein, first published
  as "Performing a Project Premortem," Harvard Business Review, 2007;
  write-up consulted: gary-klein.com/premortem), adapted in this
  skill as The Headline and the Cause
- The Situation-Behavior-Impact (SBI) feedback model (developed by
  the Center for Creative Leadership; write-up consulted:
  ccl.org/articles/leading-effectively-articles/closing-the-gap-between-intent-vs-impact-sbii),
  adapted in this skill as One Moment Each

